## DESCRIPTION
##   Continuous versus Discrete
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by nhamblet

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Classifying data)
## Date(6/3/2002)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('Mathematical Statistics')
## AuthorText1('Wackerly, Mendenhall, Scheaffer')
## EditionText1('6')
## Section1('1.1')
## Problem1('1')
## KEYWORDS('Continuous', 'Discrete','statistics', 'introduction', 'concepts')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',     'PGML.pl',
    'PGchoicemacros.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

@event = (
    "The number of voters who vote Democratic.",
    "The number of students applying to graduate schools.",
    "The number of errors found on a student's research paper.",
    "The number of customers waiting in line at the grocery store.",
    "The length of time it takes to fill up your gas tank.",
    "The length of time needed for a student to complete a homework assignment.",
    "The temperature in any given location.",
    "The distance traveled by a city bus each day."
);
@ans = (0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1);

@slice = random_subset(4, 0 .. $#event);

@dd =
    map { DropDown([ 'discrete', 'continuous' ], $ans[ $slice[$_] ]) } (0 .. 3);

@sevent = (@event[@slice]);
@sans   = (@ans[@slice]);

BEGIN_PGML

Determine whether the following examples are discrete or continuous data sets. 

a) [$sevent[0]]  [_]{$dd[0]}  

b) [$sevent[1]]  [_]{$dd[1]}  

c) [$sevent[2]]  [_]{$dd[2]}  

d) [$sevent[3]]  [_]{$dd[3]}  

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that a discrete variable is generally has a value that is a counting or whole number. A continuous variable cannot be written with a whole number and needs a fraction or a real. 

a) [$dd[0]]

b) [$dd[1]]

c) [$dd[2]]

d) [$dd[3]]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT(
    'Questions selected randomly from a larger set.  See source for full question list.'
);
ENDDOCUMENT(); 
